    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense. i think i remember my professor mentioning something about it being \"spooky\" though? like \"spooky action at a distance\" or something? why did he call it that if it's just physics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"spooky\" label was coined by Albert Einstein himself, who was initially skeptical of entanglement. He thought it was too weird, too unexplainable by classical physics. Einstein's phrase \"spooky action at a distance\" was a way of expressing his discomfort with the idea that particles could be connected in such a way that their properties could be instantaneously affected, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nBut, in a way, the \"spookiness\" of entanglement is what makes it so fascinating. It's a phenomenon that challenges our classical notions of space and time, and forces us to think about the nature of reality in a more fundamental way. It's like the idea that there's a hidden, invisible network of connections between particles, which is both exhilarating and unsettling.\n\nAnd, just like how a cleverly hidden trap can be set by a cunning troll, entanglement can be thought of as a kind of \"troll trap\" for our classical understanding of the world. It's a reminder that there's still so much we don't understand about the universe, and that the laws of physics can be more mysterious and counterintuitive than we ever imagined.",
      "source": "target",
